  ------------------------------------------------------
   CHILD COMBINATOR ( > )
   ------------------------------------------------------

   The child combinator in CSS selects ONLY the 
   DIRECT children of a parent element.

   It is written using the > symbol between selectors.

   ✔ Syntax:
       parent > child

   ✔ Meaning:
       Selects only the child elements that are 
       directly inside the parent, NOT grandchildren.

   ✔ Key Difference from Descendant Combinator:
       • Descendant (space) → selects ALL levels inside
       • Child ( > ) → selects ONLY first-level children

   ------------------------------------------------------
   Example:
       div > p {
           color: red;
       }

   What this means:
       • Select <p> elements
       • ONLY if they are DIRECT children of <div>
       • Ignore <p> elements that are nested deeper

   ------------------------------------------------------
   HTML Example Explained:

       <div>
           <p>Direct child → selected</p>

           <section>
               <p>Grandchild → NOT selected</p>
           </section>
       </div>

   ✔ Explanation:
       - The first <p> is placed directly inside <div>,
         so it MATCHES the selector div > p and turns red.

       - The second <p> is inside <section>, which is inside <div>,
         so it is a GRANDCHILD of <div>.
         Child combinator does NOT select grandchildren,
         so this <p> is NOT styled.

   ------------------------------------------------------
